                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 13.2: Schematic of greenhouse effect on Earth"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.083,
            0.885,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The air on Venus is almost entirely made up of carbon dioxide gas, which does not let the heat escape. This is called the greenhouse effect, and it makes Venus even hotter than Mercury, which is relatively closer to the Sun. On the Earth also, gases like carbon dioxide in the atmosphere trap heat by absorbing the radiation given off by the Earth, after it gets warmed by the Sun. Thus, greenhouse effect plays an important role in maintaining just the right temperature on Earth (Fig. 13.2)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.242,
            0.283,
            0.41,
            0.304
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A step further"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.242,
            0.316,
            0.561,
            0.503
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The greenhouse effect that causes a planet like Venus and Earth to trap heat does not work the same way as a greenhouse for growing plants in a cool climate. On Venus or Earth, gases like carbon dioxide in the atmosphere trap heat by absorbing the radiation given off by the Earth, after it gets warmed by the Sun. A plant greenhouse, on the other hand, traps warmed air"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
